      "heading": "systemd is PID 1 — the parent of everything",
      "narration": "Systemd is the first process the kernel starts, PID one, and it is the ancestor of everything else on the machine. Its job is to start services in the right order, keep them running, restart them when they crash, and collect their logs. You talk to it with systemctl, and the commands are mostly self-explanatory: start, stop, restart, and status. But there is one distinction on this slide that catches nearly everybody at least once, so let us make it stick. Start runs a service right now, in this moment, and that is all it does. Enable makes a service start automatically at every future boot, and it does not start it now. They are completely independent. So you can start a service, confirm it works, be satisfied, and then discover weeks later after a reboot that it never came back — because you started it but never enabled it. The reverse also happens: you enable something and wonder why nothing is running. The command that does both is enable dash dash now, and it is worth making that your default. Also notice reload versus restart: reload asks the service to re-read its configuration while continuing to serve, whereas restart stops and starts it, dropping every connection. For a busy web server that difference is very visible to your users.",
